    <section class="panel area-how how" aria-labelledby="h-how">
      <h2 id="h-how">How the numbers work</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Agents spend most of their time waiting on the model. The duty cycle is the share they spend building or running tests.</li>
        <li>With that share, the number of builds running at once follows a binomial distribution. Memory must hold the 99th-percentile count, because running out kills jobs. CPU must cover the 95th, because contention only slows things down.</li>
        <li>Each agent also holds a little memory all the time, and the system keeps some back.</li>
        <li>Slower single-thread scores stretch the serial part of every build, which raises that machine's effective duty cycle. Weak multi-thread scores stretch the parallel part.</li>
        <li>Lifetime per agent is (price + years × yearly power) ÷ agents. Machines you already own count power only. "Worth it" means it beats the 128 GB Core Ultra 7 270K Plus desktop under the same settings; "Close call" is within 25%.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Defaults come from the October 2026 research report. Mac scores are estimates converted into PassMark terms, several power figures are estimated, and used prices vary by listing. Your own duty cycle is the number that matters most, so measure it.</p>
    </section>
